1
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:10,459
Momentum sometimes rings and sometimes creeps, and we'd like to know why.

2
00:00:10,459 --> 00:00:14,076
So let's zoom in on a single direction, with curvature lambda.

3
00:00:14,076 --> 00:00:20,166
Plug in its gradient, and the weight and the running average get tangled into a two-by-two system.

4
00:00:20,166 --> 00:00:27,259
Untangle that system and everything hangs on one number, which is eta, times one minus beta, times lambda.

5
00:00:27,259 --> 00:00:31,274
The recurrence has two roots, and a step mixes their powers.

6
00:00:31,274 --> 00:00:36,200
So both roots have to stay inside the unit circle.

7
00:00:36,200 --> 00:00:37,563
Are both roots inside?

8
00:00:37,563 --> 00:00:43,576
The Schur test checks that with three conditions, which are no root at one, no root at minus one,

9
00:00:43,576 --> 00:00:45,063
and a product below one.

10
00:00:45,063 --> 00:00:51,333
Put together, they say that our one number has to lie between zero and two times one plus beta.

11
00:00:51,333 --> 00:00:56,006
Without momentum, that means eta times lambda has to stay below two.

12
00:00:56,006 --> 00:01:00,884
With beta at zero point nine, the stable range is nineteen times wider.

13
00:01:00,884 --> 00:01:06,066
But the real win from momentum is speed, not range.

14
00:01:06,066 --> 00:01:12,000
Now fix beta at one half and slide that number up from zero, while we watch the two roots on the left.

15
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:18,300
The bigger root sets the speed, and its size is what the curve on the right plots.

16
00:01:18,300 --> 00:01:24,033
At the small end, both roots are real and positive, and the slow one sits close to one.

17
00:01:24,033 --> 00:01:27,394
That's smooth but slow, and it's called overdamped.

18
00:01:27,394 --> 00:01:34,666
Slide further and the two roots collide, which is critical damping, the fastest decay without any wobble.

19
00:01:34,666 --> 00:01:41,667
Past that point they split into a complex pair, both of size root beta, and the rate goes flat into a plateau.

20
00:01:41,667 --> 00:01:44,848
Near the far end they both turn real and negative.

21
00:01:44,848 --> 00:01:51,066
Then at three, one of them reaches minus one, and the mode goes unstable.

22
00:01:51,066 --> 00:01:53,916
Start the mode at one and watch it over time.

23
00:01:53,916 --> 00:02:00,122
The overdamped run creeps toward zero, and the critical one gets there fast without ever crossing.

24
00:02:00,122 --> 00:02:07,633
The underdamped run overshoots and rings, but the ringing shrinks by root beta at every step, so it's still fast.

25
00:02:07,633 --> 00:02:12,778
So oscillation isn't the enemy here, and only the size of the roots matters.

26
00:02:12,778 --> 00:02:19,900
And when beta is zero, the two boundaries coincide and the regimes merge into one.

27
00:02:19,900 --> 00:02:21,461
Now for the real problem.

28
00:02:21,461 --> 00:02:26,958
One eta has to serve every eigenvalue, so each mode sits at its own place on this curve.

29
00:02:26,958 --> 00:02:31,705
A soft mode wants a bigger step, while a stiff one is already near the edge,

30
00:02:31,705 --> 00:02:35,366
and their positions are a factor of kappa apart.

31
00:02:35,366 --> 00:02:42,370
So tuning is a minimax game, where we make the slowest mode as fast as we can, and the plateau is the trick.

32
00:02:42,370 --> 00:02:45,159
Pick beta so the plateau covers every mode.

33
00:02:45,159 --> 00:02:50,533
Then they all converge at one rate, which is set by the square root of kappa.

34
00:02:50,533 --> 00:02:56,922
Plain gradient descent pays kappa where momentum pays root kappa, and that's the tuned heavy-ball result.

35
00:02:56,922 --> 00:02:58,322
How big a deal is that?

36
00:02:58,322 --> 00:03:00,999
At kappa twenty, forty-six steps become ten.

37
00:03:00,999 --> 00:03:06,633
At kappa one hundred, two hundred and thirty become twenty-three.

38
00:03:06,633 --> 00:03:09,589
There's one more trick, and it comes from Nesterov.

39
00:03:09,589 --> 00:03:14,227
Measure the gradient where momentum is about to take you, not where you are now.

40
00:03:14,227 --> 00:03:21,266
So we look ahead first and check the slope there, and tuned right, this provably needs only about root kappa steps.

41
00:03:21,266 --> 00:03:25,851
Be careful, though, because books and libraries write this in different ways.

42
00:03:25,851 --> 00:03:31,133
Never mix a line from one form with an update from another.

